WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.020
Glad you're with us on First Principles Daily, Episode 3. It's June 8th, 2026, Monday,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.240
a fresh First Principles tear down to start the week.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.320
De-salinated water cells for several dollars per cubic meter,

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.800
yet the thermodynamic minimum energy needed to separate the salt is worth only a few cents.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.620
The rest is everything except the membranes.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.080
Sea water holds about 35 kilograms of dissolved salt in every thousand liters,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.580
removing that salt to produce usable freshwater runs into a firm physical limit set by entropy

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:39.920
and the energy of mixing. Plants today already operate within a few multiples of that thermodynamic

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.740
floor when only energy use is measured. Delivered prices, however, sit at several dollars rather

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.540
than cents. The difference traces to capital recovery, intake and outfall structures,

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:59.200
pre-treatment steps, and brine handling. Reverse osmosis systems lead large scale sea water work

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.460
because they skip the phase change that thermal distillation requires. High pressure pumps

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:10.300
drive sea water against semi-permeable membranes that hold back the salt while water passes through.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.820
The full expense, though, starts well before any membrane experiences pressure.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:22.520
Coastal intake structures must be built large enough and screened carefully to limit harm to marine life.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.520
Which adds both construction outlays and time for approvals.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.520
Pre-treatment follows to strip out algae, suspended solids, and organic matter that would otherwise

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:38.520
clog the membranes. This stage often involves coagulants, multiple filter stages,

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.780
and sometimes dissolved air flotation, each bringing its own equipment, reagents and upkeep.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:51.760
Financing enters next. A sizeable facility requires several years of construction before any water flows,

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:58.060
so interest and required returns on the invested capital are spread across 20 or 30 years of

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:03.820
operation. Electricity for the pumps forms the main running cost after capital recovery.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:11.640
Modern isobaric recovery devices reclaim 95% or more of the pressure energy from the concentrated brine.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.680
Yet net demand still lands near 3-4 kilowatt hours for each thousand liters of output.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.580
Brine disposal creates further outlays.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:25.560
The concentrated reject must be returned to the ocean without creating pockets of excessive

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:32.260
salinity that damage local ecosystems, which calls for diffusers, extended pipelines, or extra

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:37.600
treatment. Most facilities are still erected as unique civil projects rather than standardized

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:44.180
products. Site-specific design, extended environmental reviews, and scattered supply chains for

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:50.460
vessels, piping, and pumps keep installed costs elevated. Operators have grown accustomed to each

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:56.240
of these steps because each evolved from the plants that came before. The result is a price that feels

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.300
ordinary inside the industry, even while it remains far above the physical minimum.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:07.020
The theoretical minimum energy to separate salt from seawater is the free energy of mixing.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:13.720
For typical salinity of 35 grams per liter, 25 degrees Celsius, and 50 percent recovery,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:21.000
that floor lies near 1.0 to 1.1 kilowatt hours for each thousand liters of fresh water produced.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:26.680
A rough magic wand estimate therefore sets the energy itself as the sole input.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:33.240
At 5 cents per kilowatt hour, the thermodynamic work alone would amount to roughly 5 to 6 cents

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:38.200
for every thousand liters. Actual facilities use three to four times that electricity,

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:44.480
so the energy portion already carries an idiot index of three to four relative to the absolute floor.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:51.120
Electricity however is only one slice of the total price. Large reverse osmosis installations

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.980
report overall costs between roughly one and three dollars for every thousand liters,

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.000
with capital amortization often making up 40 to 60 percent of that figure.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:06.400
If the entire plant could be assembled instantly from raw commodities at commodity prices,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:12.280
the structural steel, concrete, stainless piping, pressure vessels, membranes, and pumps

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.680
would account for perhaps 20 to 30 percent of today's installed cost.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.380
The remaining 70 to 80 percent stems from engineering, permitting, site preparation,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.960
financing across multi-year builds, and the custom integration of every subsystem.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:33.220
This yields an overall idiot index of three to five when only energy is compared

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:39.340
with the thermodynamic floor, and closer to four to ten once the complete capital and operating

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:46.420
stack is set against that same floor. Four concrete areas drive the gap. Intake and outfall works

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:52.160
are civil projects whose expense is set more by regulatory cycles than by tonnage of material.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.320
Pre-treatment and post-treatment add multiple stages sized for worst case feed water rather

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.620
than average conditions. High pressure pumps and recovery hardware, though already efficient,

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.840
still require precision machining and special alloys that carry manufacturing margins.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:14.700
The project delivery approach itself multiplies every item through stretch schedules and risk

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:21.100
allowances. None of these four items is dictated by the physics of salt removal. Each follows from

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:26.180
how the sector currently elects to construct and finance the equipment that performs the separation.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:32.280
A redesigned from first principles would start with the delivery model instead of membrane chemistry.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:38.180
Factory built modules that arrive pre-tested and are simply stacked along the shore could shrink

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:44.440
construction from years to months, cutting the capital recovery burden directly. Such modules would

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:49.780
need supply agreements that treat the plant as a repeatable item rather than a one-time civil job.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:56.680
The first units would still face site specific reviews, yet later identical units could draw on

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:02.680
earlier environmental clearances. Integrated pre-treatment that trims the number of chemical stages would

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:09.500
come next. Managing intake water quality through subsurface wells or improved screening rather than

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:15.600
heavy surface treatment would lower both capital and chemical running costs. Energy recovery hardware

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:21.080
already sits near its physical ceiling, so further savings would arise from pairing plants with

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:27.860
lower cost variable renewable power, whose surplus can be used when available. Brian handling stays

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:34.280
genuinely challenging. Useful applications such as mineral recovery or salt tolerant farming remain

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:41.320
possible, but add processing whose economics stay tied to location. Any redesign must therefore

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:47.480
accept that Brian management will keep imposing real costs and instead aim to shrink the volume and

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:52.840
variability of the reject stream through higher recovery enabled by better scale control.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:58.800
The toughest limits remain regulatory timelines and the lack of a standardized product platform.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.840
Until several jurisdictions accept modular designs with pre-cleared environmental boundaries,

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:10.960
the scheduled gains cannot be realized at scale. The arithmetic is nevertheless straightforward.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:17.300
Having capital recovery and soft costs while holding energy intensity at current levels would move

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:23.860
delivered prices, noticeably nearer to the sum of the thermodynamic floor plus unavoidable pre-treatment

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:30.300
and Brian expenses. A process whose price sits well above its thermodynamic minimum is signaling a

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:36.380
design and delivery challenge rather than an energy shortage. Examining the need for site-specific

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:41.380
multi-year construction before refining the membranes or pumps inside that construction

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:47.920
shows where the largest ratios actually reside. The first clear sign that someone is pursuing exactly

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:55.100
this approach in desalination would be a credible plan for a repeatable factory built module whose total

00:07:55.100 --> 00:08:06.360
installed cost per unit of capacity falls well below today's custom plant benchmark. That's first

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:11.380
someone who depreciated and subscribed wherever you listen. I'm Patrick and Van Coover,

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:19.040
one example or one opportunity every day. See you tomorrow. This episode used AI voice synthesis of

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.620
my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
